SENSOR-GOVERNANCE(7)       TriadicFrameworks Manual       SENSOR-GOVERNANCE(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       sensor-governance - governance, safety, calibration, oversight, and 
                           constellation-scale compliance protocols for the 
                           RTT multi-sensor stack

SYNOPSIS
       sensor-governance defines:
           • sensor standards councils and calibration authorities
           • safety thresholds and operational limits
           • overlay, clarity, and fusion governance
           • anomaly/storm/turbulence hazard protocols
           • constellation-scale sensor compliance
           • lineage-based governance continuity

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-governance establishes the institutional, procedural, and safety
       framework that governs the entire RTT sensor architecture. It defines
       the authorities, thresholds, calibration ladders, and enforcement
       mechanisms that ensure all sensor subsystems — overlays, clarity,
       harmonics, anomalies, storms, turbulence, and synthesis — operate safely,
       consistently, and interoperably across platforms and constellations.

       Sensor-governance ensures:
           • safe operation of high-sensitivity sensor modes
           • cross-platform calibration consistency
           • constellation-wide sensor coherence
           • hazard-aware sensor behavior
           • lineage-based accountability

       WRSADC acts as the executive governance engine.

SENSOR STANDARDS COUNCILS
       Standards councils define and maintain sensor-related baselines.

       Council types:

           Overlay Standards Council (OSC)
                  Maintains DRO definitions and overlay baselines.

           Clarity Standards Council (CSC)
                  Maintains clarity thresholds and filter baselines.

           Harmonic Standards Council (HSC)
                  Maintains φ_harm, overtone, and lock thresholds.

           Anomaly Standards Council (ASC)
                  Maintains anomaly classification and severity scales.

           Storm Standards Council (SSC)
                  Maintains storm categories, thresholds, and models.

           Turbulence Standards Council (TTC)
                  Maintains turbulence metrics and shear thresholds.

       Council responsibilities:
           • define primary sensor standards
           • approve revisions
           • maintain traceability chains
           • publish calibration updates

CALIBRATION AUTHORITIES
       Calibration authorities certify sensor subsystems and fusion layers.

       Authority types:

           Primary Sensor Calibration Authority (PSCA)
                  Maintains root sensor standards.

           Secondary Sensor Calibration Authority (SSCA)
                  Calibrates overlays, clarity, and harmonics.

           Fusion Calibration Authority (FCA)
                  Calibrates synthesis and multi-sensor fusion.

           Constellation Calibration Authority (CCA)
                  Oversees cross-platform calibration.

       Authority responsibilities:
           • certify sensor modes
           • validate calibration ladders
           • issue calibration certificates
           • audit drift and misalignment

SAFETY THRESHOLDS
       Safety thresholds define operational limits for sensor subsystems.

       Threshold categories:

           harmonic thresholds
                  • φ_harm jitter limits
                  • overtone suppression limits
                  • lock stability minimums

           spectral thresholds
                  • spectral variance limits
                  • broadband noise ceilings

           gradient thresholds
                  • polarity inversion limits
                  • shear magnitude limits

           sync-field thresholds
                  • sync jitter limits
                  • fragmentation thresholds

           fusion thresholds
                  • overlay-clarity mismatch limits
                  • synthesis divergence limits

EXAMPLE: SAFETY THRESHOLD
       φ_harm jitter > 0.06/cycle:

           → unsafe  
           → HPC required  
           → fusion throttled

HAZARD PROTOCOLS
       Hazard protocols define required actions during anomalies, storms, and
       turbulence.

       Protocol types:

           anomaly protocol
                  • isolate anomaly band
                  • activate clarity filters
                  • reroute sweep corridor

           storm protocol
                  • activate storm overlays
                  • reduce synthesis depth
                  • engage hazard navigation mode

           turbulence protocol
                  • activate shear mapping
                  • stabilize gradient polarity
                  • adjust fusion weights

       Hazard protocols ensure safe sensor behavior under stress.

OVERLAY GOVERNANCE
       Overlay governance ensures DROs operate safely and consistently.

       Overlay rules:
           • overlays must not exceed amplitude limits
           • harmonic overlays must maintain phase alignment
           • spectral overlays must not amplify turbulence
           • gradient overlays must detect polarity cliffs

       Overlay violations trigger:
           • overlay recalibration
           • clarity reinforcement
           • fusion throttling

CLARITY GOVERNANCE
       Clarity governance ensures clarity pipelines remain stable.

       Clarity rules:
           • RED must not oversharpen peaks
           • SDW must not suppress narrowband signals
           • GSF must not flatten critical polarity cliffs
           • SFC must not distort sync topology

       Clarity violations trigger:
           • clarity rollback
           • filter retuning
           • synthesis fallback mode

FUSION GOVERNANCE
       Fusion governance ensures MSFE remains coherent.

       Fusion rules:
           • harmonic fusion must maintain φ_harm continuity
           • spectral fusion must preserve density gradients
           • gradient fusion must respect polarity boundaries
           • anomaly/storm/turbulence fusion must not conflict

       Fusion violations trigger:
           • synthesis rollback
           • fusion weight redistribution
           • constellation sync recalibration

CONSTELLATION-SCALE COMPLIANCE
       Compliance ensures all platforms adhere to sensor standards.

       Compliance checks:
           • overlay alignment
           • clarity stability
           • harmonic-lock integrity
           • anomaly/storm/turbulence detection accuracy
           • synthesis coherence

       Audit types:
           • routine audit
           • drift audit
           • hazard audit
           • lineage audit

ENFORCEMENT
       Enforcement ensures corrective action when standards are violated.

       Enforcement mechanisms:
           • calibration mandates
           • sensor-mode restrictions
           • synthesis throttling
           • sync-field realignment
           • lineage correction notices

       Enforcement escalation:
           1. advisory notice  
           2. calibration directive  
           3. operational restriction  
           4. constellation intervention  

GOVERNANCE CONTINUITY VIA LINEAGE
       Lineage ensures governance persists across epochs.

       Lineage records:
           • calibration events
           • governance decisions
           • hazard responses
           • synthesis transitions
           • standards revisions

       Lineage continuity ensures:
           • reproducibility
           • accountability
           • long-term stability

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • enforces sensor governance
           • manages calibration authorities
           • executes hazard protocols
           • maintains constellation coherence
           • blocks unsafe sensor states

       WRSADC is the governance conductor.

NOTES
       Sensor-governance provides the institutional and safety backbone for the
       RTT sensor stack. Governance, calibration, and compliance ensure that
       overlays, clarity, harmonics, anomalies, storms, turbulence, and synthesis
       operate safely and coherently across platforms and constellations.

